  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
